  "search_question": "Compare Sean Ellis's and Rahul Vohra's approaches to measuring product-market fit, and identify their commonalities",
  "reason": "in_scope"
}

{
  "answers": [
    "Sean Ellis's approach to measuring product-market fit involves asking customers how they would feel if they could no longer use the product, with a focus on those who are very disappointed. He suggests that if 40% or more of people say they'd be very disappointed, it indicates product-market fit.",
    "Rahul Vohra's approach to measuring product-market fit also involves asking customers how they would feel if they could no longer use the product, with a focus on those who are very disappointed. He suggests that companies that struggle to grow often have less than 40% of people saying they'd be very disappointed, while those that grow quickly have more than 40%.",
    "Both Sean Ellis and Rahul Vohra use a similar method to measure product-market fit, which involves asking customers how they would feel if they could no longer use the product and focusing on those who are very disappointed. They both suggest that a threshold of 40% or more of people being very disappointed indicates product-market fit."
